// KIP/1: what a field looks like on the wire, and why it looks like that.
//
// Two Harmony components are installed SEPARATELY. A machine can hold a browser
// from one release beside an AI runtime from another, and the shell that talks
// to both is whichever one was installed last. So the format's first duty is not
// compactness: it is that a decoder built against an older or newer version of a
// message can still read the fields it knows and step over the ones it does not.
//
// That rules out the obvious encoding. A struct written as its fields back to
// back, in declaration order, is unreadable to a peer whose copy of that struct
// has one more field in the middle -- and unreadable SILENTLY, as every field
// after the insertion is read as its neighbour. So every field carries a KEY,
// every value's length is discoverable from its key alone, and a decoder that
// meets a key it does not know skips the value whole and keeps going.
//
// A field the encoder did not write is not an error either: the decoder starts
// from the type's own defaults and overwrites only what arrived. Adding a field
// to a message is therefore not a breaking change in either direction, which is
// the property the whole install story rests on.

// The three shapes a value can take, which is all a decoder needs to skip one it
// does not understand.
//
// Three rather than one per type: the decoder skipping an unknown field must
// know how far to jump, and nothing else. What the bytes MEAN is the generated
// decoder's business, and it only ever reads fields it declared.
enum IpcWire {
    // A LEB128 varint. Every integer, every Bool, every enum tag.
    Var
    // Eight bytes, little-endian. A Float, and only a Float.
    Fixed
    // A varint byte count and that many bytes. A String, a Bytes field, a nested
    // message, an array -- everything whose size is its own.
    Len
}

// A key is one varint: the field's tag in the high bits, its wire shape in the
// low two.
//
// Packing them together rather than writing two varints is what makes skipping
// an unknown field cost one read: the shape arrives with the tag that identified
// it as unknown, so the decoder never has to look further to know how far to go.
function ipcWireCode(wire: borrow IpcWire) -> Int {
    match wire {
        Var -> return 0;
        Fixed -> return 1;
        Len -> return 2;
    }
}

function ipcWireFromCode(code: Int) -> IpcWire {
    if code == 1 {
        return IpcWire.Fixed
    }
    if code == 2 {
        return IpcWire.Len
    }
    // Code 3 is unassigned. Reading it as a varint is the recoverable answer: a
    // varint is self-delimiting, so the decoder steps over the value and carries
    // on rather than losing the rest of the frame to a length it cannot find.
    return IpcWire.Var
}

function ipcKey(tag: Int, wire: borrow IpcWire) -> Int {
    // The tag is one-based, so key 0 is not a field and a run of zero bytes at
    // the end of a buffer cannot read as one.
    return (tag << 2) | ipcWireCode(wire)
}

function ipcKeyTag(key: Int) -> Int {
    return key >> 2
}

function ipcKeyWire(key: Int) -> IpcWire {
    return ipcWireFromCode(key & 3)
}
